WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.171
Imagine planning a family when every job comes with an
expiry date.

00:00:04.171 --> 00:00:06.952
Would you feel ready to have a child?

00:00:06.952 --> 00:00:10.080
Across many richer countries, people are having fewer
children.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.336
This study asks whether an insecure start to working life
can leave a lasting mark on family formation.

00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:24.275
Economists Mario Martinez Jimenez and Judit Vall Castello
study a major Spanish labour reform in nineteen eighty-four.

00:00:24.275 --> 00:00:30.965
Employers gained much greater freedom to use temporary
contracts, while existing permanent workers kept their
protections.

00:00:30.965 --> 00:00:35.982
For young people entering work, the rules of the game had
changed.

00:00:36.792 --> 00:00:40.420
Comparing temporary and permanent workers directly could be
misleading.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:46.871
Instead, the researchers compare nearby birth cohorts
reaching working age before and after the reform, across

00:00:46.871 --> 00:00:48.887
education groups with different exposure.

00:00:48.887 --> 00:00:54.128
This difference-in-differences approach asks whether
outcomes changed more for the more exposed group.

00:00:54.128 --> 00:00:58.966
It relies on how the groups would have evolved without the
reform.

00:00:59.792 --> 00:01:02.637
Employment histories reveal how early careers changed.

00:01:02.637 --> 00:01:05.889
National birth records track births and their timing.

00:01:05.889 --> 00:01:13.206
Fertility surveys add something the records cannot tell us:
whether people had the number of children they wanted.

00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:17.677
Together, these sources connect working conditions to family
decisions over time.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:24.396
Among the exposed cohorts of women with lower education, the
study estimates fifteen point two per

00:01:24.396 --> 00:01:28.819
cent fewer births per woman, and first motherhood about five
months later.

00:01:28.819 --> 00:01:32.135
Early careers became more temporary, with lower recorded
income.

00:01:32.135 --> 00:01:35.083
There was no detectable reduction in days worked.

00:01:35.083 --> 00:01:38.400
The security of work matters alongside having a job.

00:01:39.208 --> 00:01:43.504
The surveys suggest that this is partly about difficulty
realising family plans.

00:01:43.504 --> 00:01:47.799
Exposed women were more likely to report fewer children than
they wanted.

00:01:47.799 --> 00:01:54.243
Waiting for a more secure moment can mean postponing a
birth, and some postponed births may never happen.

00:01:55.083 --> 00:02:01.558
The researchers then build an economic model of work and
fertility over a woman's life.

00:02:01.558 --> 00:02:07.601
Within the model, repeated employment instability and
accumulated insecurity explain much of the decline.

00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:12.350
Simulations suggest that greater early-career stability
could recover some forgone births.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:16.235
These are model-based scenarios, rather than another policy
experiment.

00:02:17.042 --> 00:02:19.288
The message reaches beyond Spain.

00:02:19.288 --> 00:02:26.477
When evaluating labour-market reforms, policymakers should
consider their longer-term consequences for families,
alongside employment and earnings.

00:02:26.477 --> 00:02:33.666
Helping young adults find a predictable foothold in working
life may also help them realise their

00:02:33.666 --> 00:02:35.014
own family plans.

00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:41.073
When Careers Stall: Early-Career Job Insecurity and
Fertility over the Life Cycle.

00:02:41.073 --> 00:02:45.004
Research by Mario Martinez Jimenez and Judit Vall Castello.

